Replacing $x$ by $x - a$ gives $y = f(x - a)$, which shifts the graph right by $a$ units when $a > 0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\draw[->] (-2.6,0) -- (3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.0) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-1.0:2.8, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*(\x-1.2)*(\x-1.2)*(\x-1.2) + 0.5*(\x-1.2)*(\x-1.2) - 1.2*(\x-1.2) - 0.6});
\draw[->, red, thick] (0.667,-1.059) -- (1.867,-1.059);
\node[red] at (1.267,-1.62) {right $a$};
\node[gray] at (-1.6,1.55) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (1.9,1.55) {$y=f(x-a)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Replacing $y$ by $y - b$ instead gives $y = f(x) + b$, which shifts the graph up by $b$ units when $b > 0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\draw[->] (-2.8,0) -- (3.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, gray, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x - 0.6});
\draw[thick, blue, <->, domain=-2.2:1.6, samples=80, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {0.4*\x*\x*\x + 0.5*\x*\x - 1.2*\x + 0.6});
\draw[->, red, thick] (1.5,0.075) -- (1.5,1.275);
\node[red] at (2.35,0.675) {up $b$};
\node[gray] at (-1.3,-1.55) {$y=f(x)$};
\node[blue] at (-1.3,2.65) {$y=f(x)+b$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Add $3$ to the vertex's $x$-coordinate and $4$ to its $y$-coordinate.

$V'=(-1+3,-2+4)=(2,2)$.

Replace $x$ with $x-3$ and $y$ with $y-4$.

$\begin{align*}y-4&=((x-3)+1)^2-2\\y&=(x-2)^2+2\end{align*}$

The parabola opens upwards, has vertex $(2,2)$ and $y$-intercept $(0,6)$, and has no $x$-intercepts.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{translationBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-1,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.5)--(0,7.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,translationBlue,<->,domain=-0.25:4.25,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x-2)*(\x-2)+2});
\fill[translationBlue] (2,2) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[translationBlue] (0,6) circle (1.5pt);
\path (2,2) node[below right] {$V'(2,2)$};
\path (0,6) node[left] {$(0,6)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Move the vertical asymptote $3$ units right: $x=-1+3=2$.

Move the horizontal asymptote $1$ unit down: $y=2-1=1$.

The translated curve is $y=\dfrac{1}{x-2}+1$, with asymptotes $x=2$ and $y=1$.

For the sketch, $y=0$ gives $x=1$, and $x=0$ gives $y=\dfrac12$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{translationBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->,black] (-2,0)--(6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-3)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,black] (2,-3)--(2,5);
\draw[dashed,black] (-2,1)--(6,1);
\path (2,5) node[above] {$x=2$};
\path (6,1) node[right] {$y=1$};
\draw[thick,translationBlue,<->,domain=-2:1.75,samples=90,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1/(\x-2)+1});
\draw[thick,translationBlue,<->,domain=2.25:6,samples=90,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1/(\x-2)+1});
\fill[translationBlue] (1,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[translationBlue] (0,0.5) circle (1.5pt);
\path (1,0) node[below left] {$(1,0)$};
\path (0,0.5) node[above left] {$(0,\frac12)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

